(The ledger's assertion channel - docs/ledger/PROCEDURE.md §6. A
 generated program that finds a documented result wrong prints one
 `ASSERT <row>: expected <x> got <y>` line and exits 95, and the gen
 loop turns that into a wrong-value finding whose detail IS that line.

 Two halves, tested the way tests/260 tests the oracle - against the
 real capability, not through the seed-based generator, which by
 construction cannot emit this fixture (it only exits 95 when the
 compiler is actually wrong):

  1. 'the assert line in' picks the LAST ASSERT line out of a captured
     stdout, ignores lines that merely contain the marker rather than
     starting with it, copes with no trailing newline, and says so
     plainly when there is no such line at all. This is the part that
     decides whether a triage-able finding or a shrug reaches the human.
  2. A real program compiled, run under capture, exiting 95, and saved
     through the same 'finding save' call the loop makes - with the
     ASSERT line surviving all the way into classification.txt.

 The capture is read as BYTES, and there is a test for that below. A
 generated program's stdout can carry NUL bytes; a Vox text stops at the
 first one, so the text route silently dropped every finding whose
 program had printed one - three of seven, when a BUF-05 assertion was
 broken on purpose to check this whole path end to end.)

Print "--- the line scan ---".

a text called 'one assertion' is "the inbox size 11\nASSERT BUF-05: expected 4097 got 4096\n".
Print 'the assert line in' of 'one assertion'.

(Two assertions: the earlier one HELD and the program kept going, so the
 last is the one that killed it.)
a text called 'two assertions' is "ASSERT BUF-12: expected 1 got 0\nnoise\nASSERT BUF-13: expected 0 got 1\n".
Print 'the assert line in' of 'two assertions'.

(No trailing newline - a program killed mid-Print would otherwise lose
 the one line worth keeping.)
a text called 'unterminated assertion' is "noise\nASSERT BUF-03: expected 5066 got 4096".
Print 'the assert line in' of 'unterminated assertion'.

(The marker must START the line. A program that merely PRINTS the word
 must not be mistaken for one that failed an assertion.)
a text called 'mentions the marker' is "printing the text ASSERT BUF-01: expected 0 got 4\n".
Print 'the assert line in' of 'mentions the marker'.

a text called 'no assertion at all' is "the inbox size 11\nthe inbox capacity 64\n".
Print 'the assert line in' of 'no assertion at all'.

a text called 'nothing at all' is "".
Print 'the assert line in' of 'nothing at all'.

Print "--- a real program exiting 95 ---".

Set findings_dir to "./vf_ledger_findings".
a number called 'the cleanup before' is 'run shell' of "rm -rf ./vf_ledger_findings ./vf_ledger_out ./vf_ledger_bin ./vf_ledger_cerr ./vf_ledger_nul" and 5000.

a number called 'the compile result' is 'compile vox' of "." and "tests/fixtures/ledger_assertion.vox" and "./vf_ledger_bin" and "./vf_ledger_cerr".
Print "compiled: {'the compile result' is 0}".

a text called 'the verdict' is 'run program capturing' of "./vf_ledger_bin" and 5000 and "./vf_ledger_out".
Print "verdict: {'the verdict'}".
a number called 'the exit code' is 'exit code of' of 'the last status'.
Print "exit code: {'the exit code'}".

(Exactly the two lines 'fuzz gen once' runs on the exit-95 path.)
a text called 'the captured output' is 'read whole file' of "./vf_ledger_out".
a text called 'the ledger detail' is 'the assert line in' of 'the captured output'.
Print "detail: {'the ledger detail'}".

a number called 'the finding tally' is 'finding save' of "wrong-value" and 900095 and "tests/fixtures/ledger_assertion.vox" and 'the ledger detail'.
Print "finding saved: {'the finding tally' is greater than 0}".

a number called 'the category check' is 'run shell' of "test -d ./vf_ledger_findings/wrong-value/900095" and 5000.
Print "saved under wrong-value: {'the category check' is 0}".
a number called 'the detail check' is 'run shell' of "grep -q 'detail: ASSERT BUF-05: expected 4097 got 4096' ./vf_ledger_findings/wrong-value/900095/classification.txt" and 5000.
Print "classification carries the assert line: {'the detail check' is 0}".

Print "--- a capture with NUL bytes in it ---".

(printf, not Vox: a NUL cannot be spelled inside a text literal, which
 is the same reason it cannot survive one.)
a number called 'the nul capture' is 'run shell' of "printf 'noise\\n' > ./vf_ledger_nul; head -c 3000 /dev/zero >> ./vf_ledger_nul; printf '\\nASSERT BUF-13: expected 1 got 0\\n' >> ./vf_ledger_nul" and 5000.
Print "detail through the byte scan: {'the assert line in file' of \"./vf_ledger_nul\"}".

(The text route on the same bytes, to show what the byte scan is for:
 'read whole file' hands back everything up to the first NUL, so the
 line the finding needs is not in it.)
a text called 'the truncated capture' is 'read whole file' of "./vf_ledger_nul".
Print "detail through a text: {'the assert line in' of 'the truncated capture'}".

a text called 'the missing capture detail' is 'the assert line in file' of "./vf_ledger_absent".
Print "detail from a capture that is not there: {'the missing capture detail'}".

Print "--- the reserved exit band ---".

(90-99 must be unreachable as an ordinary exit code, or a program that
 passed every assertion could exit 95 by chance and be reported as
 having failed one. Checked over the whole draw rather than by reading
 it: 4000 programs' worth of tails.)
'rng seed' of 4242.
a number called 'the draws made' is 0.
a number called 'the codes in the band' is 0.
While 'the draws made' is less than 4000,
    a number called 'the raw code' is 'rng below' of 246,
    a number called 'the code' is 'the raw code',
    If 'the raw code' is greater than 89 then,
        Set 'the code' to 'the raw code' add 10.
    If 'the code' is greater than 89 and 'the code' is less than 100 then,
        Set 'the codes in the band' to 'the codes in the band' add 1.
    Set 'the draws made' to 'the draws made' add 1.
Print "exit codes drawn inside 90-99: {'the codes in the band'}".

a number called 'the cleanup after' is 'run shell' of "rm -rf ./vf_ledger_findings ./vf_ledger_out ./vf_ledger_bin ./vf_ledger_cerr ./vf_ledger_nul" and 5000.
